1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,957
On October 26, 2026,
I opened two self-made

2
00:00:03,957 --> 00:00:06,867
washer teaching models on this computer.

3
00:00:06,867 --> 00:00:10,507
Kimi K3.1 Pro promotes 3D downloads.

4
00:00:10,507 --> 00:00:15,967
I want to know when a
file is ready to deliver.

5
00:00:16,567 --> 00:00:20,097
I switch this viewer to side view,

6
00:00:20,097 --> 00:00:22,167
awaiting the
dimensions-versus-spec result.

7
00:00:22,167 --> 00:00:28,867
The closed-mesh result is still folded
away. What can it actually guarantee?

8
00:00:28,867 --> 00:00:35,847
When the hole's section appears, will
its narrowest point meet the requirement?

9
00:00:36,467 --> 00:00:41,400
In one viewer, I first put both
self-made files at comparable angles.

10
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:46,567
Put the spec, source files and check
results together to see the difference.

11
00:00:46,567 --> 00:00:51,267
Use the same spec to decide whether to
keep a received file or send it back.

12
00:00:51,267 --> 00:00:55,017
What step separates a beautiful
3D preview from a usable part?

13
00:01:00,633 --> 00:01:04,022
I open the spec beside
both teaching models,

14
00:01:04,022 --> 00:01:07,000
lining up required sizes and model data.

15
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:12,700
The new Kimi version, released October
24 in Taipei time, includes 3D Make.

16
00:01:12,700 --> 00:01:18,000
Its 3D Make lets you preview and
edit online, and download assets.

17
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:21,933
3D printing assets are an official use,

18
00:01:21,933 --> 00:01:25,267
but this washer's delivery
spec is still unset.

19
00:01:25,267 --> 00:01:31,633
Full weights are promised before November
4, separate from today's asset files.

20
00:01:31,633 --> 00:01:37,473
Check this washer's asset format and
purpose; “downloadable” isn't acceptance.

21
00:01:38,100 --> 00:01:43,033
It reported a score of 92.7 on
a 3D reconstruction benchmark.

22
00:01:43,033 --> 00:01:48,367
That was with tools, reconstructing a
3D shape from images at several angles.

23
00:01:48,367 --> 00:01:55,107
This open-model test randomly sampled
1,000 files from a set of 17,900.

24
00:01:55,733 --> 00:01:59,010
The metric measures how
well the reconstructed

25
00:01:59,010 --> 00:02:01,467
shape overlaps the target shape.

26
00:02:01,467 --> 00:02:05,833
It isn't the share of usable parts
after printing a hundred of them.

27
00:02:05,833 --> 00:02:10,993
Back to this washer: what specific
requirement is missing before acceptance?

28
00:02:11,600 --> 00:02:16,933
A rod must pass through the center; the
outer ring must keep a contact surface.

29
00:02:16,933 --> 00:02:25,900
“Washer-like” can mean different parts
to both sides, even if it looks good.

30
00:02:25,900 --> 00:02:32,480
That difference may hide in a preview.
A written spec gives us a comparison.

31
00:02:33,100 --> 00:02:39,100
Its 40-millimeter outer diameter fixes
the space this teaching washer occupies.

32
00:02:39,100 --> 00:02:46,167
The nominal 16-millimeter hole is a design
target, not measured minimum clearance.

33
00:02:46,167 --> 00:02:50,277
Its 5-millimeter thickness is the distance
from the bottom surface to the top.

34
00:02:50,900 --> 00:02:55,667
These numbers go into a spec file,
delivered with both STL files.

35
00:02:55,667 --> 00:03:02,233
Units, outer diameter, hole diameter and
height have fields for unambiguous edits.

36
00:03:02,233 --> 00:03:07,623
You're changing your requirements.
The checker must read the same ones.

37
00:03:08,233 --> 00:03:13,667
If that 40 is just a coordinate
value, what makes it millimeters?

38
00:03:13,667 --> 00:03:20,767
STL has no unit label. The external spec
and import settings fix its meaning.

39
00:03:20,767 --> 00:03:26,637
Wrong units resize the whole part.
Fitting on screen isn't enough.

40
00:03:27,267 --> 00:03:32,233
Both files opened here are STL files
containing actual triangle data.

41
00:03:32,233 --> 00:03:38,367
Both use the same coordinate
orientation and this millimeter spec.

42
00:03:38,367 --> 00:03:44,817
Both face the same part spec, letting
us compare the specified through-hole.

43
00:03:45,433 --> 00:03:52,533
I deliberately filled one teaching model's
center as a delivery counterexample.

44
00:03:52,533 --> 00:03:54,930
It's no Kimi generation error.

45
00:03:54,930 --> 00:04:00,067
The same outer shape tests
if we asked about a hole.

46
00:04:00,067 --> 00:04:07,197
Controlling the filled-hole difference
makes an outer-size comparison possible.

47
00:04:07,800 --> 00:04:15,600
When a model arrives, you may first
measure its length, width and height.

48
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:20,000
If that were enough, it should reject
the example with the filled center too.

49
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:24,320
We now put both models, with the
same outer shape, through that check.

50
00:04:24,933 --> 00:04:31,067
Both measure 40 by 40 by 5 for
the bounding box we specified.

51
00:04:31,067 --> 00:04:38,567
Bounds enclose the outermost coordinates;
central material won't resize them.

52
00:04:38,567 --> 00:04:45,587
The ruler got the outer size right,
but hasn't answered our hole question.

53
00:04:46,200 --> 00:04:49,767
Would checking whether the mesh
is closed be more reliable?

54
00:04:49,767 --> 00:04:55,067
This check asks whether neighboring faces
correctly meet at each triangle edge.

55
00:04:55,067 --> 00:05:00,847
Neither file has boundary edges. That
alone still can't decide acceptance.

56
00:05:01,467 --> 00:05:07,500
A washer can have a through-hole and still
have a complete surface around that space.

57
00:05:07,500 --> 00:05:13,800
Outer wall joins top, top joins the hole's
inner wall, then back along the bottom.

58
00:05:13,800 --> 00:05:20,570
“Closed” means no surface gaps; it
does not require a solid center.

59
00:05:21,200 --> 00:05:27,667
The model with a hole uses 512 triangles;
the filled-hole example uses 256.

60
00:05:27,667 --> 00:05:32,752
Having twice as many faces only
tells us how this mesh was divided;

61
00:05:32,752 --> 00:05:34,767
it isn't a quality score.

62
00:05:34,767 --> 00:05:41,327
Bounds and closure missed the central
passage. We need to check somewhere else.

63
00:05:41,933 --> 00:05:48,233
The specified central hole runs through
the thickness, from bottom to top.

64
00:05:48,233 --> 00:05:54,033
A hole elsewhere, or a surface
circle, can't replace that passage.

65
00:05:54,033 --> 00:06:00,893
Both teaching models have bottoms at
zero and tops at the specified thickness.

66
00:06:01,500 --> 00:06:07,967
Unzip the teaching files together, then
open the included viewer in a browser.

67
00:06:07,967 --> 00:06:15,200
Switch top and side views to view outer
size and the central passage separately.

68
00:06:15,200 --> 00:06:23,250
Keep the spec beside the original files;
read triangles with the same program.

69
00:06:23,867 --> 00:06:29,867
Open a terminal in the teaching folder
and paste the complete command on screen.

70
00:06:29,867 --> 00:06:37,417
It reads the teaching model with a
hole and the external spec we just saw.

71
00:06:38,033 --> 00:06:40,541
The checker compares
the file's coordinates

72
00:06:40,541 --> 00:06:43,153
and triangles with the same spec.

73
00:06:43,767 --> 00:06:48,367
Paste this command, switching input to
the deliberately filled-hole example.

74
00:06:48,367 --> 00:06:56,177
The program and spec stay put; only
the model being checked changes.

75
00:06:56,800 --> 00:07:01,660
Equal sizes but different results let
us trace the source of the difference.

76
00:07:02,267 --> 00:07:06,667
Both pass the external checks.
Are triangles blocking the center?

77
00:07:06,667 --> 00:07:10,380
Inside the hole, the program samples

78
00:07:10,380 --> 00:07:13,500
triangle intersections
along the thickness.

79
00:07:13,500 --> 00:07:21,750
Finite samples give blocked-ray counts,
not guarantees for arbitrary hole shapes.

80
00:07:22,367 --> 00:07:26,667
Not one test ray in the teaching
model with a hole hits material.

81
00:07:26,667 --> 00:07:30,333
In the filled-hole counterexample,
every test ray is blocked.

82
00:07:30,333 --> 00:07:38,103
The opening's same-sized but unusable
file now has a specific rejection reason.

83
00:07:38,733 --> 00:07:44,300
At half height, the model with a hole
leaves an inner and an outer contour.

84
00:07:44,300 --> 00:07:49,633
Inside is empty; the filled example has
just the outer contour and a solid center.

85
00:07:49,633 --> 00:07:51,464
At other heights,

86
00:07:51,464 --> 00:07:54,983
the same empty-versus-filled
center difference remains.

87
00:07:55,600 --> 00:08:00,933
Material volume also confirms
that extra piece in the center.

88
00:08:00,933 --> 00:08:07,367
The filled-hole example has much more
volume, yet its bounds stay the same size.

89
00:08:07,367 --> 00:08:13,477
That blocked-center example also
fails this spec's volume condition.

90
00:08:14,100 --> 00:08:19,667
Does an empty center mean its narrowest
point meets the nominal hole diameter?

91
00:08:19,667 --> 00:08:25,300
64 straight segments approximate a circle,
with vertices at the specified radius.

92
00:08:25,300 --> 00:08:32,280
The edge between two vertices lies closer
to the center than the circular arc.

93
00:08:32,900 --> 00:08:36,871
The narrowest diameter is
about 15.98 millimeters,

94
00:08:36,871 --> 00:08:39,650
slightly below the nominal size.

95
00:08:40,267 --> 00:08:46,317
The gap is under 0.02 millimeters,
right where the rod must pass.

96
00:08:46,933 --> 00:08:52,403
Check the through-hole at its narrowest
point; having a hole is not enough.

97
00:08:53,033 --> 00:08:58,243
Require minimum clearance to
reach the nominal hole diameter,

98
00:08:58,243 --> 00:09:01,200
and this model goes back.

99
00:09:01,200 --> 00:09:07,100
That measurement puts the narrowest
point below the new requirement.

100
00:09:07,100 --> 00:09:10,009
Tighten this washer's
purpose requirements,

101
00:09:10,009 --> 00:09:14,580
and its old limited pass won't carry over.

102
00:09:15,200 --> 00:09:21,767
How do we state the added minimum
clearance so the recipient can check it?

103
00:09:21,767 --> 00:09:24,421
Delivery notes: the finished part needs

104
00:09:24,421 --> 00:09:27,500
minimum clearance of at
least 16 millimeters.

105
00:09:27,500 --> 00:09:33,000
That's a separate written requirement, not
a field implemented in the original spec.

106
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:39,240
The checker didn't test this new
condition. Its pass cannot answer it.

107
00:09:39,867 --> 00:09:47,767
This pass covers only the specified
axis, dimensions, mesh edges and volume.

108
00:09:47,767 --> 00:09:50,495
Beyond the arbitrary hole
shapes mentioned earlier,

109
00:09:50,495 --> 00:09:53,600
self-intersections and minimum wall
thickness throughout are unchecked.

110
00:09:53,600 --> 00:10:00,940
Slicing, materials, load capacity and
whether a real print works remain ahead.

111
00:10:01,567 --> 00:10:07,900
Back to Kimi downloads: this checker
reads only text STL, not images directly.

112
00:10:07,900 --> 00:10:14,167
For another mesh format, you'll need
a tool that can read it correctly.

113
00:10:14,167 --> 00:10:19,127
Renaming the extension STL doesn't
convert the contents into triangle data.

114
00:10:19,733 --> 00:10:24,873
To keep editing the hole, what else
does the package need besides this mesh?

115
00:10:25,500 --> 00:10:30,667
Previews show the overall shape; mesh
files let you inspect the actual surface.

116
00:10:30,667 --> 00:10:37,400
To keep editing hole diameter, also get
a source file preserving how to edit it.

117
00:10:37,400 --> 00:10:44,710
A mesh like STL doesn't automatically
retain how you originally drew the circle.

118
00:10:45,333 --> 00:10:51,733
I haven't tested which file
extensions Kimi actually exports.

119
00:10:51,733 --> 00:10:59,733
“Downloadable printing assets” isn't
a verified STL export tutorial.

120
00:10:59,733 --> 00:11:07,313
First check account permissions and
actual files, then choose the next tool.

121
00:11:07,933 --> 00:11:11,140
I'll hand over the
model files we just saw,

122
00:11:11,140 --> 00:11:14,000
the same-version spec and
the check results together.

123
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:20,133
If the original file changes, can
these check results still go with it?

124
00:11:20,133 --> 00:11:27,267
Change the source file, and old results
stay with the old file, not the new one.

125
00:11:27,267 --> 00:11:33,747
Senders can explain what they did;
recipients have something to verify again.

126
00:11:34,367 --> 00:11:40,967
Return the filled-hole example with its
results as concrete grounds for revision.

127
00:11:40,967 --> 00:11:45,797
We can hand over the model with
a hole and limited results,

128
00:11:45,797 --> 00:11:47,867
but no untested manufacturing guarantee.

129
00:11:47,867 --> 00:11:52,521
If the recipient adds that
minimum-clearance requirement,

130
00:11:52,521 --> 00:11:55,892
give the independent
measurement comparison.

131
00:11:56,500 --> 00:12:02,567
Good looks invite a download. A clear
purpose decides what to check afterward.

132
00:12:02,567 --> 00:12:06,307
A file meeting your spec,
with results traced to it,

133
00:12:06,307 --> 00:12:08,533
lets you say where it's usable.

134
00:12:08,533 --> 00:12:14,333
This washer must deliver an
answer the next person can verify.
